    "chunk_size_bytes": 1850,
    "chunk_text": "Hon. Deputy Speaker, contract labour has been rapidly spreading at a mass scale threatening job security in Sri Lanka. It is estimated that only 25 per cent of the workforce including public sector employees are in regular employment and this has been brought to the notice of the National Labour Advisory Council by a number of trade unions, and most trade unions are of the view that all workers, whether they are permanent, temporary or casual, shall be directly employed and absolute responsibility for the payment of wages and all statutory and other dues of such workers shall rest with the principal employer. There have been recommendations about this by the ILO itself. The National Labour Advisory Council had highlighted in 2006 the need to frame a legislation to prevent workers under contract labour being deprived of their right dues provided and recognized by our laws and practices.\n\nSir, the then Attorney-General had expressed an opinion in this regard by her letter dated 13th of January, 2011 to the Secretary to the Ministry of Labour Relations and Productivity Promotion. This was in 2011 in relation to an earlier Bill and the wordings are almost the same. It refers to a consultation that had been held with the Senior Legal Adviser of the Ministry, which states, I quote:\n\n\u201cDuring the aforesaid consultation it transpired that the purpose of this amendment is to safeguard the regular workforce since there\n\nis an unhealthy trend to minimize the regular workforce by Employers who get integral parts of the business performed by other persons who in turn employ workers to execute them. It is suggested that the definition of the term 'disguised employment relationship' be further elaborated...\"\n\n\u0dc0\u0da9\u0dcf \u008a\u00bbf %\u0006\u0dba\u0da7 H*\u0dc0\u0da0\u0db1\u0dba; \u0d9a\u0dc5 \u0086,\u007f >\u0dba\u0dbd\u0dcf \u00d4Z\u0db4Z,\u0db8\u0dcf \u0dd9\u0dba\u0005\u0da2\u0db1\u0dcf \u0d9a\u0dbb\u0db1\u0dc0\u0dcf.\n\nHe states :"
