    "chunk_text": "is an unhealthy trend to minimize the regular workforce by Employers who get integral parts of the business performed by other persons who in turn employ workers to execute them. It is suggested that the definition of the term 'disguised employment relationship' be further elaborated...\"\n\n\u0dc0\u0da9\u0dcf \u008a\u00bbf %\u0006\u0dba\u0da7 H*\u0dc0\u0da0\u0db1\u0dba; \u0d9a\u0dc5 \u0086,\u007f >\u0dba\u0dbd\u0dcf \u00d4Z\u0db4Z,\u0db8\u0dcf \u0dd9\u0dba\u0005\u0da2\u0db1\u0dcf \u0d9a\u0dbb\u0db1\u0dc0\u0dcf.\n\nHe states :\n\n\u201cFurther, it is suggested to incorporate a prohibition clause on disguised employment relationships which have the effect of depriving such workers of their entitlements under the law, after the aforesaid definition of the term, in order to address the concerns raised in the ILO recommendation 198.\"\n\nThe Attorney-General continues to state, I quote:\n\n\u201cIt is also prudent to retain a clause in line with Sec. 59A(3)(a) of the principal enactment, although it is proposed to be repealed by the draft Bill, to make Employers who, despite the prohibition on disguised employment relationships, resort to such arrangements to get any work executed, liable to pay wages to such workers in accordance with the provision of this Ordinance.\n\nSubject to the above suggestions, Section 59A(2) may be retained with appropriate amendments to empower the Commissioner to also give directives to Employers to pay the wages due to workers for work already executed under a disguised employment relationship, in addition to giving written directions to Employers to refrain from having work executed under a contract or arrangement which falls within the definition of disguised employment relationship.\n\nIn view of the above observations, please make the necessary amendments to the draft Bill and submit same for reconsideration in order to issue the Certificate of Constitutionality.\""
