    "chunk_index": 106,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "8b8e50cbb21cd979e54db171dc6670c6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1521,
    "chunk_text": "I am happy that the Hambantota Port is now coming into operation. We had an idle port and it certainly is a step in the right direction to commercialize it instead of just having a white elephant, saying that we have a port but not having a single container handled via that. We see vibrancy coming through that. That is the way we need to take our shipping industry forward. The Oluvil Port also needs to be put into commercialization. We have some other ports. The Galle Port,too, should be looked at from a more commercial-oriented way. I call upon the Hon. Minister to ensure that officials with a thinking of commercialization put into each port and a target given to them with productivity as the most important aspect, competitiveness as its key word as well as ensuring there is automation, technology development, speed of handling and a modernization approach to be brought into Sri Lankan ports.\n\nSir, I do not want to take more time labouring over the same point, but only want to say that we also need to increase our productivity. The JCT, which roughly handles only 23 or 24 containers an hour, needs to be improved to handle 28 to 29 containers. Then, you have the CICT which can be taken as a barometer of real productivity and which handles 28 to 29 containers an hour. That also should be increased so that we will have a quicker throughput. With that, there will be a competitive element coming in so that we can compete within our region and also ensure that the ships are turned around much quickly."
  },
